  "assumptions": [
    "Illustration: a made-up two-setting loss bowl with simulated batch noise, not a real model trained on real data.",
    "Loss = (2.5 x^2 + 0.5 z^2) / 2, steep along x. The surface is drawn at 0.4 times the loss. Plain steps multiply x by 1 - 2.5 x rate, so x overshoots above rate 0.4 and swings grow above rate 0.8.",
    "Each step is -rate x (slope + noise). Noise is random, centred on zero, size 0.8 x sqrt((64 - b) / (63 b)) for batch b, like sampling b of 64 points; exactly zero at 64. A step lowers the loss only on average.",
    "Both arrows are steps drawn at 4 times their real length, one scale for both, tilted along the surface. Mini-batch step is the actual move; Full-data step is -rate x true slope. Arrows hide when the run ends; the step length readout then shows 0.",
    "A run ends after 40 steps (finished), or diverged when the loss passes 5 or the ball leaves the drawn square. Off-surface points are drawn at the rim. Noise comes from a fixed seed, so Reset replays it; a new run uses the next noise values.",
    "While playing, the ball takes one step every 5 host ticks (6 steps per second at speed 1). Take one step moves it exactly once. The trail holds the start plus up to 40 steps."
  ]
}